<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Ramillies</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1706">1706</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Ramillies · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Ramillies was fought on 23 May 1706 near the village of Ramillies in Brabant, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>. An allied army under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-churchill-1st-duke-of-marlborough">Duke of Marlborough</a> routed the French and Bavarian forces of Marshal Villeroi. Within weeks most of the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, fell to the allies, who recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> claimant Charles III there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-ramillies.jpg" alt="Battle of Ramillies" width="320" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rijksmuseum · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_slag_bij_Ramillies_tussen_de_Fransen_en_de_Geallieerden,_23_mei_1706,_SK-A-4061.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Grand Alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> had followed an indecisive campaign in 1705. Although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> that year, they abandoned their campaign on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>, stalled in the Spanish Netherlands and were defeated in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> wanted peace, but on reasonable terms, and so the French took the offensive in 1706. In April Marshal Vendôme defeated the Austrians at the Battle of Calcinato, and Louis urged Villeroi to attack in the Spanish Netherlands to win a fair peace.</p><p>Marlborough had hoped to take his army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> or back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>, but the monarchs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> refused to let their contingents march that far, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> would commit none of their own troops. He left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> on 9 May, writing that he had little hope of doing anything considerable unless the French chose to fight.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Villeroi marched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a> with about 60,000 men, and Marlborough assembled some 62,000 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a>. The armies met on dry ground between the rivers Mehaigne and Petite Gheete. Villeroi held Taviers, Ramillies, Offus and Autre-Eglise, with cavalry on the open plain between Taviers and Ramillies. At about 13:00 the batteries opened, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> troops took Franquenée and then Taviers in close fighting.</p><p>The Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> led English troops against Offus and Autre-Eglise, and Villeroi shifted battalions from his centre to meet them. Marlborough then moved Orkney's second line south, hiding the move behind a fold in the land, and pressed the attack on Ramillies. Around 16:30 the weight of the Allied cavalry began to tell. Danish squadrons under the Duke of Württemberg swept round the French right and into the flank of the Maison du Roi. The French and Bavarian army broke in under four hours, and Marlborough halted the pursuit after midnight near Meldert, 19 km from the field.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Villeroi lost 52 guns and his pontoon train. Casualty figures vary widely between authorities. Chandler gives about 12,000 French dead and wounded and some 7,000 prisoners, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> says the French lost twenty thousand men. Marshal Villars called the defeat the most shameful, humiliating and disastrous of routs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a> fell on 25 May and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> three days later. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>, Oudenaarde and, on 6 June, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> followed, each proclaiming the Archduke Charles as sovereign. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> recalled Vendôme from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to command in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. With Prince Eugene's success at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, 1706 brought the largest loss of territory and resources Louis suffered in the war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Ramillies” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the Habsburgs Brabant became a main theatre of the Eighty Years' War, with the Spanish Fury at Antwerp in 1576, Alexander Farnese's siege of Antwerp in 1584–1585, and in 1706 the Duke of Marlborough's victory at the <b>Battle of Ramillies</b> gave the allies control of the duchy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the War of the Spanish Succession John Churchill, Duke of Marlborough, won at <b>Ramillies</b> in 1706, Oudenarde in 1708 and Malplaquet in 1709, and the Treaty of Utrecht (1713) gave Britain Gibraltar, Minorca, Newfoundland and Nova Scotia and the asiento to supply enslaved Africans to Spanish America.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Military</a></li></ul></section>
